#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171891 :question/id #uuid "bccee2b9-f937-5df5-b3cf-4e45096c9ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814 :topic/difficulty 0.4298039530009263 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428386 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: